  "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
